WEBVTT

00:15:58.542 --> 00:16:01.393
EP: You know, then
Rachel Botsman talked about

00:16:01.417 --> 00:16:04.434
how trust is an active
or confident engagement

00:16:04.458 --> 00:16:05.726
with the unknown.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:08.226
By definition, trust is a leap of faith.

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:11.518
Therefore, if you think
transparency leads to trust,

00:16:11.542 --> 00:16:13.351
probably not always -- sometimes,

00:16:13.375 --> 00:16:16.018
but at other times,
transparency leads to surveillance.

00:16:16.042 --> 00:16:18.018
Surveillance is the opposite of trust.

00:16:18.042 --> 00:16:20.434
Trust is what you live with,
what you don't know.

00:16:20.458 --> 00:16:22.976
If you always have to know,
you're not trusting.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.893
AG: This was such a surprise to me
when Rachel captured this.

00:16:25.917 --> 00:16:29.018
I had always thought of transparency
as a driver of trust, right?

00:16:29.042 --> 00:16:30.309
So if you want somebody